Title: Nigeria: How Emissions Reduction Can Unlock Government Revenues
Abstract: The wasteful flaring and venting of gas associated with oil and gas production and processing is a persistent problem, leading to lost taxable income and reduced energy efficiency and access, adverse health impacts, and global warming. Nigeria is of particular interest since it is Africa’s leading oil producer and the world’s eighth highest flarer. The paper documents the scale and value of Nigeria’s flaring, the reasons for its persistence, and the opportunity to capture the lost value. In 2024, the value of this wasted flare gas was around USD1.6 billion (and more if all exported at world prices); together with the value of vented gas, the lost value is the equivalent to some one percent of GDP. The paper places in the broader context the findings from a companion paper that analyzes the continued excess flaring of five super-emitter flares operated by international oil companies. Gaps in regulation, enforcement, measurement, and transparency are identified. Building on reforms undertaken by the Nigerian government, the paper sets out practical solutions to capture the value of the wasted gas through policy changes, including independent measurement, greater transparency, gas monetization, and fiscal and regulatory measures. Focus is placed on the opportunity for a methane fee and rebate scheme to incentivize companies to reduce flaring. The potential roles for various stakeholders are set out. A proposed next step is to develop a detailed government revenue model for flare and vent gas monetization options, potentially with IMF support, and in consultation with government, companies, and other stakeholders.
